{"t":1791421182,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"9e37e86b-cdd8-4fda-89d3-df377f74c978","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-08T00:00:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 080000\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n800 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nFine tuned hourly trends. No major changes.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Quiescent weather expected through the end of the week.\n\n- 2) Remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias arrive late this \n     weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiescent weather expected through the end of\nthe week.\n\nVery few if any clouds were evident as of early evening thanks \nto sprawling high pressure over the area. Winds will become \nlight over land tonight. Temperatures tonight will be milder \nthan recent nights given light south winds.\n\nA dry cold front will approach from the west late tonight, then\ncross the region early Thursday morning. Winds will shift to the\nwest and northwest at 10 to 15 mph with gusts of 20 to 25 mph,\nperhaps occasional/peak gusts near 30 mph near higher terrain.\nDespite the passage of the front, downsloping winds likely\npushing temperatures well into the 70s to near 80 for most.\n\nSeasonable (albeit slightly cooler) temperatures and dry\nconditions will then persist under high pressure Friday through\nFriday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remnants of Tropical Storm Isaias arrive late this \nweekend.\n\nAs of today, the National Hurricane Center is now issuing advisories \non Tropical Storm Isaias down in the Bay of Campeche. This Tropical \ncyclone will need to be monitored closely in the coming days in \nregards to potential impacts to our region late this weekend. There \nremains a great deal of uncertainty, owing to a complex upper-level \npattern ahead of it. A cutoff low over the southern Plains and an \narea of high pressure over Florida will act as the primary steering \nmechanisms in the coming days. The big questions are going to be... \nwhere does it go, and how fast does it get there? A track directly\nover our area is highly uncertain at this time. However, it does\nseem likely to track somewhere up into the MS River Valley and \nthen eventually up towards the Great Lakes/OH Valley as it \ntransitions into a non-tropical low.\n\nAt present, the most likely impacts to our region will be rain. \nHowever, there isn't a great concern for flooding at this time, as \nthe system should be progressive and much of the region could use a \ngood soaking rain to help with drought relief. That being said, this \nsystem is still several days out, so we will be monitoring any \nrainfall trends closely as the time draws closer. There is also a \nnon-zero, but very small, chance that some severe weather could \noccur as a result of the remnants of Isaias. However, again, this \nwill greatly depend on how the upper-level pattern evolves and \ninteracts with Isaias as it moves into our region. The main \nthreat, if it materializes, would be the non-zero threat of a \nquick tornado on Sunday afternoon/evening (mainly near S MD). \n\nAll of this to say, we just simply don't know if Isaias will bring \nany significant impacts our way. However, as is the case with any \ntropical cyclone, it will be important to stay tuned to the forecast \nover the next few days. Monitor hurricanes.gov for details on \nIsaias.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nMain wx change next 24 hrs is wind shift Thu morning behind a \ndry cold frontal passage. Otherwise, P6SM SKC through 21Z Fri. \nThen, increasing high clouds Fri night.\n\nVFR conditions are expected Saturday as high pressure remains \ndominant over the region. Winds will be out of the S to SE on \nSaturday at 5 to 10 knots. \n\nMore uncertainty going into Sunday, but sub-VFR conditions are \npossible if we do manage to get any rain associated with the area of \nlow pressure over the southwestern Gulf of America.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSoutherly winds are fcst to strengthen this evening across the\nwaters tonight. A Small Craft Advisory remains in effect for \ntonight for the main channel of the Chesapeake Bay up to Pooles \nIsland, and the lower tidal Potomac River. Winds Thursday shift \nto the west and northwest with SCA gusts expected over the upper\ntidal Potomac and upper Chesapeake Bay/Baltimore Harbor. Winds \ndrop below SCA by late Thu afternoon through Friday.\n\nWinds are expected to remain below SCA criteria on Saturday as high \npressure remains dominant over the region. However, by Saturday \nevening, we could see a brief burst of southerly channeling winds \nthat come close to criteria. Confidence is low at this time. \n\nWinds will be on the increase on Sunday ahead of the remnants of \nIsaias, with high-end SCA conditions possible. SCAs seem likely for \nmuch of the day and into Sunday night.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTide levels may increase during the second half of the weekend\ndepending on the exact direction and strength of the winds as\nthe remnants of Isaias approach.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory from 6 AM to 6 PM EDT Thursday for ANZ530-\n     535-538.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EDT Thursday for ANZ531.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EDT Thursday for ANZ532-533-\n     537.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 8 AM EDT Thursday for ANZ534-543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/DHOF/CJL\nAVIATION...LFR/DHOF/CJL\nMARINE...LFR/DHOF/CJL\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791421133,"days":{"2026-10-07":{"hi":null,"lo":57,"pop":0,"short":"Clear"},"2026-10-08":{"hi":78,"lo":54,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-09":{"hi":75,"lo":56,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-10":{"hi":75,"lo":60,"pop":94,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-11":{"hi":68,"lo":64,"pop":95,